// REQUIRES: gdb, lldb, host-linux-amd64 || host-linux-arm64
// RUN: %gopp build %S/Inputs/ops.gopp -o %t
// RUN: timeout 30 gdb -batch -nx -q -x %S/Inputs/ops.gdb %t 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=GDB
// RUN: timeout 30 lldb --no-lldbinit -b -s %S/Inputs/ops.lldb %t 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LLDB
// P04.23, both sides. A frame in an operator method is named in the readable
// form of appendix A.9, `main.Vec2.+(main.Vec2)` (the overload is told apart
// by its parameter types), and a plain function keeps `main.f`. The choice is
// not `Vec2::operator+`: it is the one spelling all Go++ frames use
// (docs/design/debug-info.md section "Operator frames").
//
// GDB: Breakpoint 1, main.Vec2.+(main.Vec2) (a=..., b=...) at {{.*}}ops.gopp:15
// GDB: #0 {{ *}}main.Vec2.+(main.Vec2) (a=..., b=...) at {{.*}}ops.gopp:15
// GDB-NEXT: #1 {{.*}} in main.f (a=..., b=...) at {{.*}}ops.gopp:26
// GDB: a = {X = 1, Y = 2}
// GDB: b = {X = 3, Y = 4}
// GDB: Breakpoint 2.{{[0-9]}}, main.Vec2.*(float64) (a=..., k=2)
// GDB-NOT: _GP
//
// LLDB: frame #0: {{.*}}`main.Vec2.+(a=(X = 1, Y = 2), b=(X = 3, Y = 4)) at ops.gopp:15
// LLDB: frame #1: {{.*}}`main.f(a=(X = 1, Y = 2), b=(X = 3, Y = 4)) at ops.gopp:26
// LLDB: `main.Vec2.*(a=(X = 4, Y = 6), k=2) at ops.gopp:20
// LLDB-NOT: _GP
